WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.070 --> 00:00:04.540
It's great to welcome to the program today,
David Courtright, who is a university of North

00:00:04.540 --> 00:00:07.360
Florida historian and addiction expert.

00:00:07.360 --> 00:00:13.230
Also author of the the age of addiction, how
bad habits became big business.

00:00:13.230 --> 00:00:14.410
So great to have you on today.

00:00:14.410 --> 00:00:15.610
I appreciate your time.

00:00:15.610 --> 00:00:17.480
Well David, it's great to be here.

00:00:17.480 --> 00:00:22.690
So let's start with maybe just defining some
of the terms we're using when we talk about

00:00:22.690 --> 00:00:24.789
a addiction.

00:00:24.789 --> 00:00:30.230
Are we talking about it in a, in a sort of
way that more approximates what the medical

00:00:30.230 --> 00:00:32.009
community sees as addiction?

00:00:32.009 --> 00:00:37.330
Or are we talking about something else that
may be is, uh, more related to cultural norms

00:00:37.330 --> 00:00:39.480
or expectations or social norms?

00:00:39.480 --> 00:00:42.010
What do we mean by addiction in this sense?

00:00:42.010 --> 00:00:43.670
Actually, we're talking about both.

00:00:43.670 --> 00:00:47.629
We're talking about addiction in a medical
sense and we're also talking about the social

00:00:47.629 --> 00:00:49.250
fact of addiction.

00:00:49.250 --> 00:00:54.370
That more and more people are describing more
and more things as being addictive, you know,

00:00:54.370 --> 00:01:00.450
and in many ways the big question is whether
that's hype or whether there really is something,

00:01:00.450 --> 00:01:06.689
a medical and in a public health way, very
important about these novel forms of addiction.

00:01:06.689 --> 00:01:11.990
The one area that probably immediately comes
to people's minds when we talk about the business

00:01:11.990 --> 00:01:18.110
of addiction is social media and smartphones
and these variable reward systems that function

00:01:18.110 --> 00:01:20.810
very much like, like slot machines.

00:01:20.810 --> 00:01:23.680
But you actually are looking beyond that,
right?

00:01:23.680 --> 00:01:26.650
I mean you're, you're uh, looking at other
industries as well,

00:01:26.650 --> 00:01:27.650
right?

00:01:27.650 --> 00:01:32.460
I was, um, actually it was as much a matter
of personal curiosity as anything.

00:01:32.460 --> 00:01:37.680
I started working in this field 40 years ago
and when I started out, if you put played

00:01:37.680 --> 00:01:41.850
a word association game, what do you think
of when you think of addiction?

00:01:41.850 --> 00:01:44.920
People would say heroin or some other drug.

00:01:44.920 --> 00:01:47.240
And that was pretty much yet.

00:01:47.240 --> 00:01:50.640
Now you could get any answer or any one of
several answers.

00:01:50.640 --> 00:01:54.869
You could get porn, you could get social media,
you could get food addiction.

00:01:54.869 --> 00:01:57.910
Uh, there are lots and lots of addictions
out there.

00:01:57.910 --> 00:02:04.570
And so the basic question is what led to such
a proliferation of addictions and what does

00:02:04.570 --> 00:02:09.040
it do or have to do with our, our economic
and social systems.

00:02:09.040 --> 00:02:10.810
So let's start thinking about that.

00:02:10.810 --> 00:02:14.290
The, there's this term limbic capitalism that
you use.

00:02:14.290 --> 00:02:17.060
Can we first talk a little bit about what
you mean by that?

00:02:17.060 --> 00:02:19.520
Cause it's so key to understanding your perspective.

00:02:19.520 --> 00:02:20.520
Right.

00:02:20.520 --> 00:02:26.050
And so I was looking for a common denominator
and what I found is that there are in fact

00:02:26.050 --> 00:02:30.840
many different kinds of businesses that have
a common strategy.

00:02:30.840 --> 00:02:35.700
And that strategy is to provide products and
services that produce a quick hit of brain

00:02:35.700 --> 00:02:37.600
reward.

00:02:37.600 --> 00:02:44.060
And that's really the key because all ultimately
most of their profits come from only a handful

00:02:44.060 --> 00:02:49.740
of their customers and they really depend
on those, um, heavy regular users.

00:02:49.740 --> 00:02:56.660
And at the limit, uh, at the extreme, uh,
I think it's fair to call those users addicts.

00:02:56.660 --> 00:03:02.380
And can we apply that to, so I mean, I recently
had a conversation with a caller about the

00:03:02.380 --> 00:03:07.260
concept of the American dream and there was
this idea that you could even go, I mean,

00:03:07.260 --> 00:03:11.270
on the one hand you might have something as
nitty gritty as the Facebook algorithm, but

00:03:11.270 --> 00:03:17.180
then on the other hand you could make the
case that the idea of the American dream of

00:03:17.180 --> 00:03:23.680
a home ownership sometimes even if it's beyond
one's means of having the latest car and technology

00:03:23.680 --> 00:03:29.930
and whatever, that that can also generate
a form of what some might call addiction or

00:03:29.930 --> 00:03:32.910
addiction to consumption culture or something
